//? Title: Sørensen–Dice Similarity: Shared Byte Bigrams, Multiplicity and Lost Order
//? Subtitle: Preserve the five original word pairs, trace each consumed occurrence, and test the limits of the score
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given two strings, we want a small score describing how much adjacent local
structure they share. Repeated pieces must count correctly, and a high score
must not be confused with string equality or semantic equivalence. The
Sørensen–Dice coefficient normalizes multiset bigram overlap into a similarity
between zero and one. We first expose the actual shared pair in night/nacht,
then reproduce all five original word-pair scores. Native count and consumption
APIs reveal why aa/aaaa scores one half, why different strings can score one,
and why one minus Dice is not a metric. Literal-byte and short-string controls
make the encoding convention explicit. A repeated-byte family checks the
closed forms for Dice, multiset Jaccard and occurrence-level work without
claiming those counts measure hash probes or elapsed time.
)

\toc

> Begin with one shared pair

The string night has four adjacent byte pairs: ni, ig, gh, ht. The string
nacht has na, ac, ch, ht. Only ht is shared. Each side has four occurrences,
so their multiset intersection has size one and

$$
D(\texttt{night},\texttt{nacht})=\frac{2\times1}{4+4}=\frac14.
$$
\alttext(Dice similarity of night and nacht is twice one shared occurrence divided by four plus four, which equals one quarter.)

The native trace builds left counts, then looks up right occurrences in input
order. A successful lookup consumes one remaining left occurrence. Its rows
are sorted by an unsigned two-byte key; its events retain the actual right
positions and before/after counts. Nothing is recovered by replaying the
algorithm. This first input has no repeated keys, so its one successful
consumption is especially easy to check.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dice_shared_bigram.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::dice_coefficient_trace("night","nacht");
  if(t.overlap.shared!=1 || t.events.size()!=4 || !t.events[3].matched || t.events[3].key!=0x6874)return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(80,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1.3;box.height=.9;box.font_size=23;
  d.text({3,3},"night / nacht: one shared occurrence among eight",25)
   .text({-1.9,1.2},t.left,23).text({-1.9,-1.2},t.right,23);
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i) {
    box.fill=i==3&&t.events[i].matched?dm::graphics::RGB{220,252,231}:dm::graphics::RGB{241,245,249};
    d.node(std::format("a{}",i),{2.0*i,1.2},t.left.substr(i,2),box)
     .node(std::format("b{}",i),{2.0*i,-1.2},t.right.substr(i,2),box);
  }
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke match;match.color={22,163,74};match.width=3;
  d.edge("a3","b3","consume once",match)
   .text({3,-3},std::format("Left {}; right {}; shared {}; Dice = {:.2f}",t.overlap.left_bigrams,t.overlap.right_bigrams,t.overlap.shared,t.overlap.coefficient),23)
   .save_svg("build/dice_shared_bigram.svg");
  std::print("| bigram | left count | right count | shared count |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& row:t.counts)std::print("| {}{} | {} | {} | {} |\n",char(row.key>>8),char(row.key&255),row.left_count,row.right_count,row.shared_count);
  std::print("\n| right offset | bigram | remaining before | remaining after | consumed |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& e:t.events)std::print("| {} | {}{} | {} | {} | {} |\n",e.right_offset,char(e.key>>8),char(e.key&255),e.remaining_before,e.remaining_after,e.matched);
  std::print("\nActual left updates {}; right lookups {}; matches {}; misses {}.\n",t.work.left_updates,t.work.right_lookups,t.work.matches,t.work.misses);
}
