> Derive the multiset normalization

For each possible byte pair g, let c_a(g) and c_b(g) be its occurrence counts.
Use A and B for their totals, I for the intersection and U for the union:

$$
A=\sum_g c_a(g),\quad B=\sum_g c_b(g),\quad
I=\sum_g\min(c_a(g),c_b(g)),\quad
U=\sum_g\max(c_a(g),c_b(g))=A+B-I.
$$
\alttext(A and B are total occurrence counts; the intersection sums per-key minima and the union sums per-key maxima, equalling A plus B minus I.)

A byte string of length k contributes max(k−1,0) occurrences, without boundary
padding. When A+B is positive, Dice and multiset Jaccard are

$$
D=\frac{2I}{A+B},\qquad J=\frac I{A+B-I},\qquad
D=\frac{2J}{1+J},\qquad J=\frac D{2-D}.
$$
\alttext(Dice is twice the intersection over the sum; multiset Jaccard is the intersection over the union. Dice equals two J over one plus J, and J equals D over two minus D.)

Because I≤min(A,B), the exact ratio lies between zero and one. For positive
combined size, score one means equal occurrence counts at every key; it does
not recover global character order. The same fact follows by rewriting

$$
D=1-\frac{\sum_g|c_a(g)-c_b(g)|}{A+B}.
$$
\alttext(Dice is one minus the absolute count difference summed over keys and divided by the combined occurrence count.)

For nonzero I, treating I/B as precision and I/A as recall gives D as their
harmonic mean. The algebra concerns these counted features, without asserting
that either string is a correct label or that the result is a match probability.
The Dice/Jaccard transform is increasing, so it preserves pair rankings when
both scores use the same multiset features and the same empty convention.
Using sets of distinct bigrams would change the features and the answers.

Two zero-sized bigram bags need an explicit convention. The established scalar
API gives one for identical strings, including empty and one-byte strings,
and zero for any other comparison involving a string shorter than two bytes.
In particular a/b scores zero although both bigram bags are empty. The trace
retains that string-level convention. The count-only API cannot know the
original strings, so its empty_equal argument explicitly chooses zero or one.

[Dice's 1945 paper](https://doi.org/10.2307/1932409) introduced an ecological
coincidence index with the normalization 2h/(a+b). The byte-bigram construction
here is a later lexical use, not a claim about that paper's original algorithm.
[Kondrak, Marcu and Knight](https://aclanthology.org/N03-2016/) use character
bigrams for word similarity; datamunge specifies literal bytes and multiplicity
explicitly. The historical name Sørensen–Dice does not imply Unicode handling.

> Retain the five original word pairs

The original scores span zero to one: night/nacht shares ht; france/french
shares fr and nc; healed/sealed shares ea, al, le and ed. The identical and
disjoint controls reach the two endpoints. The new trace and the established
scalar call must agree on every row. Jaccard below uses maximum multiplicities
for the union, not the number of distinct keys.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/dice_original_pairs.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::string>> inputs{{"night","nacht"},{"france","french"},{"healed","sealed"},{"night","night"},{"abc","xyz"}};
  std::vector<std::string> labels;std::vector<double> scores;
  std::print("| original pair | A | B | intersection | multiset union | Dice | Jaccard |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto& [a,b]:inputs) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::dice_coefficient_trace(a,b);const auto& s=t.overlap;
    if(s.coefficient!=dm::algorithms::dice_coefficient(a,b))return 1;
    labels.push_back(a+"/"+b);scores.push_back(s.coefficient);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",labels.back(),s.left_bigrams,s.right_bigrams,s.shared,s.union_count,s.coefficient,s.jaccard);
  }
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.barplot(scores,labels,"Dice",{37,99,235})
   .title("The five original bigram-overlap examples").x_label("literal byte-string pair")
   .y_label("multiset Dice coefficient").y_limits(0,1.18).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/dice_original_pairs.svg");
}
```
