> Implementation, checks and limits

The canonical core now packs each unsigned byte pair into a 16-bit key and
uses size_t occurrence counts. It reserves capacity for at most 65,536 left keys,
rather than constructing a temporary substring key for each update or reserving in
proportion to a long input beyond the finite byte alphabet. Counts remain exact
integers; normalized scores use binary64 rounding. Hashing gives
expected O(|a|+|b|) scalar work and O(min(|a|,65536)) auxiliary storage.
Adversarial collision behavior can increase hash-table work. The rich trace
additionally sorts K distinct union keys in O(K log(K+1)) and owns both input
strings and one event per right occurrence. Its total storage is
O(|a|+|b|+K), with K≤65,536. Scalar identical/short inputs retain their early
return; the trace scans even those inputs so that its reported work and rows
are actual. Default complete occurrence cap is 1,000,000; zero disables only
that cap. Count-sum arithmetic remains checked, and failures return no partial
trace. Neither the count-only test above nor these bounds claim that multi-
gigabyte strings were materialised.

Independent tests compare sorted token-list intersections/unions and a separate
unconsumed-token scan against 29,282 exhaustive small ASCII/binary pairs and
2,000 seeded byte-string pairs. A bijection renaming all bytes preserves the
score. A 131,072-byte control contains every one of the 65,536 byte pairs and
checks unsigned sorting, counts and complete matching. A larger unary control
checks 100,000 successful and 200,000 failed consumptions. Count-only tests
exercise size_t boundaries without enormous allocation. Original scores,
short conventions, case/space/NUL/UTF-8 controls, equal-feature collisions,
triangle failure, exact caps, ownership and overflow/error precedence are
checked separately. No external dataset is needed for these finite controls.

The result measures shared local byte structure. It is useful as an explicitly
chosen feature score, but cannot establish string identity, Unicode equivalence,
meaning, edit distance or a metric geometry. Preserve multiplicities and choose
any preprocessing before constructing the byte strings; the score itself makes
none of those decisions.

> References

1. L. R. Dice (1945), “Measures of the Amount of Ecologic Association Between Species”, Ecology 26(3), 297–302. [Original paper DOI](https://doi.org/10.2307/1932409), [primary paper scan](https://biocomparison.ucoz.ru/_ld/0/86_dice_1945.pdf). Source of the coincidence-index normalization; the literal-byte multiset construction and short-string conventions are specified here.
2. G. Kondrak, D. Marcu and K. Knight (2003), “Cognates Can Improve Statistical Translation Models”, Companion Volume of HLT-NAACL 2003, Short Papers, 46–48. [Primary paper and metadata](https://aclanthology.org/N03-2016/). Describes bigram-based lexical Dice; this report does not reproduce its translation experiments or infer a language-independent matching threshold.
